xtend([subtype_name, subtype_suffix])\n        mime_types.extend([\n            '%s/%s' % (type_, subtype_name),\n            '%s/%s' % (type_, subtype_suffix)\n        ])\n\n    # As a last resort, if no lexer feels responsible, and\n    # the subtype contains 'json', take the JSON lexer\n    if 'json' in subtype:\n        lexer_names.append('json')\n\n    # Try to resolve the right lexer.\n    lexer = None\n    for mime_type in mime_types:\n        try:\n            lexer = pygments.lexers.get_lexer_for_mimetype(mime_type)\n            break\n        except ClassNotFound:\n            pass\n    else:\n        for name in lexer_names:\n            try:\n                lexer = pygments.lexers.get_lexer_by_name(name)\n            except ClassNotFound:\n                pass\n\n    if explicit_json and body and (not lexer or isinstance(lexer, TextLexer)):\n        # JSON response with an incorrect Content-Type?\n        try:\n            json.loads(body)  # FIXME: the body also gets parsed in json.py\n        except ValueError:\n            pass  # Nope\n        else:\n            lexer = pygments.lexers.get_lexer_by_name('json')\n\n    return lexer\n```\n\nYes, this matches httpie's actual code. The module imports `pygments.lexers` somewhere (maybe `import pygments.lexers` \u2014 the snippet shows `import pygments.lexer` which might be a typo in the extraction, but the actual code has `import pygments.lexers`).\n\nNow, for the tests, I need to be careful about state pollution. The function is pure (no global state modification), so cleanup isn't really needed. But the instructions say to clean up after tests and use monkeypatch/pytest-mock if appropriate. Since get_lexer is pure, no monkeypatching needed. However, maybe I could use monkeypatch to make tests robust against pygments version differences? E.g., monkeypatch `pygments.lexers.get_lexer_for_mimetype`? But the function references `pygments.lexers.get_lexer_for_mimetype` via the module attribute `pygments.lexers` \u2014 monkeypatching `pygments.lexers.get_lexer_for_mimetype` would work since it's looked up at call time.\n\nHmm, but monkeypatching pygments would make the test less about real behavior. I think using real pygments is better, and assertions verify real postconditions. But there's risk: if the test environment's pygments resolves 'application/vnd.api+json' directly (newer versions?), the test still passes. Let me double-check whether any pygments version handles '+json' in mimetype lookup... \n\nLooking at pygments source history: `get_lexer_for_mimetype` has been exact-match forever. There's `find_lexer_class_for_filename` which does fnmatch. I'm fairly confident mimetype lookup is exact. But wait \u2014 actually, I recall in pygments there's this in `get_lexer_for_mimetype`... no fuzzy. OK.\n\nActually, hold on. Let me reconsider: in pygments 2.14+, there's `_iter_found_lexers` and the mimetype lookup. Still exact match. OK.\n\nBut there's another subtlety: `pygments.lexers.get_lexer_by_name('plain')` \u2014 TextLexer aliases: ['text', 'verbatim', 'plain']. Yes.\n\nAnd `get_lexer_by_name('json')` \u2192 JsonLexer (name 'json'). Yes.\n\n`get_lexer_for_mimetype('application/json')` \u2192 JsonLexer (mimetypes ['application/json']). Yes.\n\n`get_lexer_for_mimetype('text/plain')` \u2192 TextLexer (mimetypes ['text/plain', ...]). TextLexer.mimetypes = ['text/plain']. Yes.\n\n`get_lexer_for_mimetype('application/xml')` \u2192 XmlLexer (mimetypes includes 'application/xml', 'text/xml', ...). Yes.\n\nNow, one concern: `get_lexer_for_mimetype('text/foo-bar-baz')` \u2014 could any lexer match? No.\n\nAnother concern: for mime 'x-foo/plain', is there any lexer with mimetype 'x-foo/plain'? No.\n\nOK. Now let me also think about whether `get_lexer` might be importable. The test file will be placed presumably in the httpie repo tests directory. Import: `from httpie.output.formatters.colors import get_lexer`. \n\nAlso, importing httpie.output.formatters.colors requires py