strictly needed.\n\nLet me also consider: the `else` clause of the for loop (line 140-141) executes only when no break. Covered by tests 3, 4, 8.\n\nNow, about the condition on line 147: `explicit_json and body and (not lexer or isinstance(lexer, TextLexer))`. Branches:\n- explicit_json False \u2192 covered by tests 1-4 (default False).\n- explicit_json True, body empty \u2192 body falsy \u2192 short-circuit. Could add: `get_lexer('text/plain', explicit_json=True, body='')` \u2192 returns TextLexer. Covers the `body` falsy branch.\n- explicit_json True, body truthy, lexer found non-Text \u2192 covered by test 7.\n- explicit_json True, body truthy, lexer is TextLexer \u2192 test 5.\n- explicit_json True, body truthy, lexer None \u2192 test 8.\n\nFor thoroughness, add test for body='' case.\n\nNow, about imports in the test file. The function is in `httpie.output.formatters.colors`. Let me use `get_info` to check the actual module structure. The tool `get_info` returns information about a symbol. Let me call it for `get_lexer` and maybe for the module.\n\nActually, the instructions say \"Use the get_info tool function as necessary.\" Let me call get_info on 'httpie.output.formatters.colors.get_lexer' or 'get_lexer' to see what it returns. I'll make a couple of independent calls in one block.\n\nLet me think about what the actual httpie code looks like (from the repo):\n\n```python\nimport json\nfrom typing import Optional, Type\n\nimport pygments.lexer\nfrom pygments.lexer import Lexer\nfrom pygments.lexers.special import TextLexer\nfrom pygments.util import ClassNotFound\n\n\ndef get_lexer(\n    mime: str,\n    explicit_json=False,\n    body=''\n) -> Optional[Type[Lexer]]:\n    # Build candidate mime type and lexer names.\n    mime_types, lexer_names = [mime], []\n    type_, subtype = mime.split('/', 1)\n    if '+' not in subtype:\n        lexer_names.append(subtype)\n    else:\n        subtype_name, subtype_suffix = subtype.split('+', 1)\n        lexer_names.extend([subtype_name, subtype_suffix])\n        mime_types.extend([\n            '%s/%s' % (type_, subtype_name),\n            '%s/%s' % (type_, subtype_suffix)\n        ])\n\n    # As a last resort, if no lexer feels responsible, and\n    # the subtype contains 'json', take the JSON lexer\n    if 'json' in subtype:\n        lexer_names.append('json')\n\n    # Try to resolve the right lexer.\n    lexer = None\n    for mime_type in mime_types:\n        try:\n            lexer = pygments.lexers.get_lexer_for_mimetype(mime_type)\n            break\n        except ClassNotFound:\n            pass\n    else:\n        for name in lexer_names:\n            try:\n                lexer = pygments.lexers.get_lexer_by_name(name)\n            except ClassNotFound:\n                pass\n\n    if explicit_json and body and (not lexer or isinstance(lexer, TextLexer)):\n        # JSON response with an incorrect Content-Type?\n        try:\n            json.loads(body)  # FIXME: the body also gets parsed in json.py\n        except ValueError:\n            pass  # Nope\n        else:\n            lexer = pygments.lexers.get_lexer_by_name('json')\n\n    return lexer\n```\n\nYes, this matches httpie's actual code. The module imports `pygments.lexers` somewhere (maybe `import pygments.lexers` \u2014 the snippet shows `import pygments.lexer` which might be a typo in the extraction, but the actual code has `import pygments.lexers`).\n\nNow, for the tests, I need to be careful about state pollution. The function is pure (no global state modification), so cleanup isn't really needed. But the instructions say to clean up after tests and use monkeypatch/pytest-mock if appropriate. Since get_lexer is pure, no monkeypatching needed. However, maybe I could use monkeypatch to make tests robust against pygments version differences? E.g., monkeypatch `pygments.lexers.get_lexer_for_mimetype`? But the function references `pygments.lexers.get_lexer_for_mimetype` via the module attribute `pygments.lexers` \u